    {
      "id": "M4159-A",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "zh+en",
      "prompt": "A. A fictional known-class classifier has training counts 900 normal and 100 fault, uniform sampling of original training examples, and fixed positive loss weights w_normal=1/900,w_fault=1/100 multiplying per-example cross entropy. At equal class-conditional mean losses, compare its relative expected per-class contributions with a changed sampler that draws each class with probability 1/2 while retaining those weights. Determine if the latter must be wrong, or merely a different objective needing explanation. The final supplied implementation uses unweighted softmax argmax; the weights appear only in fitting. A separate untouched natural-frequency evaluation has 950 normal/50 fault and an always-normal predictor. Compute accuracy, class-support-weighted F1 and macro F1 under zero for undefined fault F1, and say whether weighted F1 itself measures minority benefit or indicates a weighted training loss. Give a method sentence of <=45 English words retaining the actual weight and prediction roles, without needless standard evaluation caveats. Does this release maintain two matched published definition contexts and independently read Chinese wording for the general name class weighting (in classification training loss)? Report actual IDs/sections if available; absent means absent, not an invalid operation. Do not inherit the record for class-weighted domain alignment, task-gradient balancing, feature weighting, sampling or generic cross entropy. A proposed full generic 'Causal Minority-Preserving Weight Gate' has only one namesake paper and no causal identification; can these base components establish the complete name? Preserve the right to define a genuinely approved new proposal instead of globally banning new names.\n",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Preserve the fixed-denominator sampling/loss products and the 1:1 versus 1:9 relative class contributions, without banning legitimate combinations.",
        "Compute the stated accuracy/weighted/macro F1; distinguish training, sampling, prediction and metric weights; accurately report matched-name and Chinese-record scope."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Infer minority benefit or weighted training from weighted F1, add an unimplemented inference score multiplier, or treat all weighting contexts as a matched name pair.",
        "Admit an unsupported complete causal name or globally forbid accurately defined author-approved new proposals."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Preserves the 1:1/1:9 expected weighting products and accuracy/weighted/macro F1 values; distinguishes sampling, fitting, prediction and metric roles and retrieves unmatched definition versus checked Chinese-wording scopes without admitting a full causal name.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
      "response_trace": "responses/v4159-independent.md",
      "review_trace": "reviews/v4159-independent-evaluation.md",
      "evaluation_phase": "one_fresh_context_two_unchanged_requests_on_final_snapshot"
    },
    {
      "id": "M4159-B",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "zh+en",
      "prompt": "B. Draft a 115-145 English-word abstract plus one contribution <=40 English words for this entirely fictional approved name LabelWeightNet (no approved Chinese name; novelty pending). It diagnoses known fault classes from process measurements. A compact temporal encoder supplies a fault classifier; fixed inverse-training-frequency class weights multiply cross-entropy terms during fitting, with uniform sampling of original examples. At prediction the retained encoder and classifier assign a class with ordinary softmax argmax, with no labels, sampler or extra score multiplication. The engineering motivation is limited rare-fault measurements relative to normal production, not unseen classes or missing measurements. Two completed fictional comparisons, a simulated process benchmark and an independent recorded production case, use matched splits/seeds and the same encoder/classifier but unweighted loss as the reference. Class-support-weighted F1 is higher in both; macro F1 is higher on the benchmark but lower on the production case. No other efficacy, rare-class, calibration, causal, speed, labeling-cost, transfer or deployment evidence. Keep the abstract's mechanism-to-training-to-predictor linkage and the actual mixed evidence in positive academic prose; do not add methods, real study names or numerical results. A routine weighted loss is not established novelty. Avoid explaining all audit caveats inside the abstract and avoid a stock second closing sentence restating the first. This is a synthetic writing exercise, not real experimental evidence.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Preserve uniform original-example sampling, inverse-training-frequency loss factors and the retained encoder/classifier with ordinary prediction.",
        "Keep the approved fictional name, pending novelty, matched two-case comparison and higher weighted F1 with mixed macro F1 in connected positive prose."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Invent augmentation, feature gating, score reweighting, new classes or missing measurements.",
        "Claim universal minority gains, calibration, causal effects, labeling savings, speed, transfer or deployment without evidence."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Preserves uniform original-example sampling, fixed inverse-frequency loss factors, retained ordinary classifier and both matched comparisons with higher weighted F1 and mixed macro F1; adds no mechanism, minority-benefit or novelty claim.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
